# CLI diagnostic output implementation

Status: planned

Related design: `docs/test-runner-service-design.md`

## Purpose

Make `dwarfspec run` render live test failures and errors in a standard
single-line diagnostic format that editor problem matchers can consume.
Projects select the format with `settings.error_format` in
`tests/dwarfspec/config.lua`.

This checklist is the implementation completion contract. A phase is complete
only when every subtask and exit-gate item in that phase is checked and its
evidence is recorded in the evidence ledger.

## Scope boundary

Included:

- project configuration and validation for `settings.error_format`;
- immutable identifiers for the supported formats;
- structured source locations on `problem.recorded` events;
- MSBuild, GCC/Clang, and ESLint Compact diagnostic rendering;
- external CLI integration while a run is being polled;
- DwarfSpec unit tests for configuration, events, formatting, and runner
  behavior;
- documentation and example VS Code task configuration.

Excluded:

- changing the `dfhack-run` transport;
- formatting events inside the DFHack service or command adapters;
- changing test scheduling, cleanup, persistence, or exit-code behavior;
- managing, retaining, grouping, collapsing, or deduplicating VS Code Problems;
- implementing a background or watch-task problem matcher;
- automated tests of VS Code, the Problems panel, or `.vscode/tasks.json`;
- changing the separately planned in-game runner UI.

The editor is responsible for clearing problems from the previous task run.
DwarfSpec will not emit lifecycle markers or run-specific diagnostic owners to
manage stale Problems.

## Locked implementation contract

☐ Keep structured service events presentation-neutral.
☐ Perform all format selection and diagnostic rendering in the external
  command.
☐ Configure the format with `settings.error_format` in
  `tests/dwarfspec/config.lua`.
☐ Support exactly these serialized configuration values:
  `msbuild`, `gcc`, and `eslint`.
☐ Treat `gcc` as the shared GCC/Clang-compatible format.
☐ Default `settings.error_format` to `msbuild` when it is omitted.
☐ Represent the supported values with an immutable enum created by
  `dwarfspec.immutable_enum.define`.
☐ Apply `error_format` only to `problem.recorded` diagnostics.
☐ Preserve the current human-readable queue, run, test progress, cleanup,
  scheduler, and final-summary lines.
☐ Emit one physical output line for each formatted diagnostic.
☐ Retain the complete original problem message and trace in the structured
  event journal and persisted result.
☐ Never invent a file or line number when no source location is known.
☐ Fall back to a human-readable problem line when no matchable source location
  is available.
☐ Preserve current process exit codes, cleanup behavior, result persistence,
  acknowledgement, and recovery behavior.
☐ Do not add a CLI option or environment variable for selecting the format as
  part of this work.

## Canonical output contracts

The examples below define the required shapes. Exact message escaping is
specified in Phase 3.

MSBuild, selected by `error_format='msbuild'`:

```text
D:\project\tests\example.ds.lua(12,4): error DS1001: suite example: expected true
```

GCC/Clang, selected by `error_format='gcc'`:

```text
D:/project/tests/example.ds.lua:12:4: error: suite example: expected true
```

ESLint Compact, selected by `error_format='eslint'`:

```text
tests/example.ds.lua: line 12, col 4, Error - suite example: expected true (dwarfspec)
```

The VS Code pairing is:

| DwarfSpec format | VS Code problem matcher |
|---|---|
| `msbuild` | `$mscompile` |
| `gcc` | `$gcc` from the Microsoft C/C++ extension |
| `eslint` | `$eslint-compact` |

MSBuild and GCC/Clang output use absolute paths. ESLint Compact uses canonical
project-relative paths because its matcher resolves files relative to the
workspace folder.

## Global engineering rules

☐ Add LuaDoc comments to every new or changed Lua function, method, and
  class-like module or table, including private helpers.
☐ Use immutable enums instead of loose string comparisons for the supported
  error-format identifiers.
☐ Keep source-location extraction separate from output-format rendering.
☐ Keep formatter functions deterministic and free of filesystem writes or
  DFHack dependencies.
☐ Preserve unrelated worktree changes.
☐ Record only fresh verification evidence in this checklist.

## Phase 0: Baseline and diagnostic fixtures

Goal: characterize the current event and Busted callback data before changing
the public output.

☐ Confirm the workspace is the intended DwarfSpec repository before editing
  implementation files.
☐ Record the current result of `tools/Check-Lua.ps1`.
☐ Record the current result of `tools/Run-UnitTests.ps1`.
☐ Add representative problem fixtures for an assertion failure, a test error,
  a hook or file error, and a problem without a source location.
☐ Characterize the Busted trace values received by `baseTestFailure`,
  `baseTestError`, and `baseError`, including table and string forms.
☐ Include Windows and Unix-like source paths, a project root containing
  spaces, a missing column, a multiline message, and ANSI/control characters
  in the fixture set.
☐ Record the current human-readable `problem.recorded` output so intentional
  changes are distinguishable from unrelated regressions.

Phase 0 exit gate:

☐ Baseline syntax and unit results are recorded in the evidence ledger.
☐ Fixtures describe DwarfSpec and Busted data only; they contain no mocked
  VS Code APIs or Problems-panel assertions.
☐ The source-location precedence needed by the fixtures is explicit before
  implementation begins.

## Phase 1: Configuration and immutable identifiers

Goal: make the selected format a validated project setting available to the
external command.

☐ Add an immutable error-format enum module with `MSBUILD`, `GCC`, and `ESLINT`
  members whose values are `msbuild`, `gcc`, and `eslint`.
☐ Export the default format as the enum's `MSBUILD` value rather than a
  duplicated string.
☐ Refactor `dwarfspec.config` to load and validate the external settings needed
  by `list` and `run` without executing the configuration module more than once
  per command.
☐ Preserve discovery configuration and its current default and override
  behavior.
☐ Validate `settings.error_format` as one of the immutable enum values.
☐ Reject an empty, non-string, or unsupported `settings.error_format` with the
  exact configuration path in the diagnostic.
☐ Update the in-process extension settings validator to accept the same
  setting while leaving it unused by the service and host.
☐ Ensure the external command and in-process loader do not disagree about
  whether a configuration file is valid.
☐ Pass the resolved enum value into the external runner options.
☐ Keep format selection project-local so simultaneous commands from different
  projects can choose different formats independently.

Phase 1 exit gate:

☐ Unit tests cover the default and all three accepted values.
☐ Unit tests cover invalid types, empty values, unsupported values, and
  unknown settings.
☐ Unit tests prove discovery and error-format settings are loaded together
  without changing discovery precedence.
☐ Unit tests prove separate project roots resolve independent formats.
☐ Lua syntax checks and the full unit suite pass.

## Phase 2: Structured problem source locations

Goal: give the external formatter structured location data instead of making
it recover locations from already formatted CLI text.

☐ Extend the `problem.recorded` payload contract with optional
  `source_identity`, `line`, and `column` fields.
☐ Keep `source_identity` canonical and project-relative when the location is
  inside the owning project.
☐ Validate nonempty source identities and positive integer line and column
  values when those fields are present.
☐ Require `line` whenever `column` is present.
☐ Extract structured Busted trace fields before considering rendered trace
  text.
☐ Prefer a location in the selected test file over DwarfSpec, Busted, Lua, or
  cleanup implementation frames.
☐ Use the test element's source location as the fallback when the trace does
  not provide a more precise project-owned location.
☐ Add a bounded fallback parser only for callback forms that Busted exposes as
  strings.
☐ Normalize both slash styles without requiring the source file to exist on
  the external command's filesystem at formatting time.
☐ Leave all location fields absent when no trustworthy project-owned source
  location can be determined.
☐ Preserve the original `message` and `trace` values in the event payload.
☐ Ensure cursor reads, result persistence, and run-history inspection retain
  the new location fields unchanged.

Phase 2 exit gate:

☐ Unit tests cover structured traces, string traces, element fallback, missing
  locations, mixed separators, paths with spaces, and internal-frame
  rejection.
☐ Event-schema tests cover valid optional locations and every invalid
  field combination.
☐ Unit tests prove the original message and trace remain available after
  adding structured locations.
☐ No output format names or VS Code concepts appear in the service-side
  source-location extractor.
☐ Lua syntax checks and the full unit suite pass.

## Phase 3: Standard diagnostic formatters

Goal: render a normalized problem into each supported one-line contract.

☐ Introduce a formatter boundary that accepts an immutable error-format value,
  project root, and structured problem payload.
☐ Implement the canonical MSBuild output shape.
☐ Implement the canonical GCC/Clang output shape.
☐ Implement the canonical ESLint Compact output shape.
☐ Map assertion failures to stable MSBuild code `DS1001`.
☐ Map test, hook, and file errors to stable MSBuild code `DS1002`.
☐ Render both problem kinds with error severity so a failed DwarfSpec test is
  always reported as a problem.
☐ Default a missing column to column 1 only when a valid source and line are
  present.
☐ Resolve MSBuild and GCC/Clang source identities against the owning project
  root to produce absolute paths.
☐ Preserve canonical project-relative paths for ESLint Compact output.
☐ Convert embedded CR, LF, tab, and other control characters in displayed
  fields to safe spaces.
☐ Remove ANSI escape sequences before rendering a diagnostic.
☐ Collapse whitespace introduced by sanitization without changing the
  structured event or persisted result.
☐ Include the full test or hook name in the diagnostic message.
☐ Preserve colons, parentheses, spaces, and non-ASCII text that do not violate
  the selected one-line grammar.
☐ Return the existing human-readable problem shape when the payload has no
  trustworthy source and line.

Phase 3 exit gate:

☐ Unit tests assert the exact output for all three formats.
☐ Unit tests cover both problem kinds, both path styles, spaces in paths,
  missing columns, multiline messages, ANSI escapes, non-ASCII text, and
  location-less fallback.
☐ Unit tests prove every matchable problem produces exactly one physical line.
☐ Unit tests verify only DwarfSpec formatter behavior; they do not instantiate
  VS Code or assert Problems-panel behavior.
☐ Lua syntax checks and the full unit suite pass.

## Phase 4: Live-progress runner integration

Goal: apply the project-selected formatter while the external command consumes
structured events.

☐ Pass the resolved error format and project root through
  `dwarfspec.runner.run`.
☐ Update event formatting so `problem.recorded` uses the selected diagnostic
  formatter.
☐ Keep all non-problem event formatting byte-for-byte compatible unless a
  separate defect requires an explicit change.
☐ Emit a problem diagnostic as soon as its validated event is consumed.
☐ Ensure cursor retry and repeated polling cannot print the same event twice.
☐ Ensure formatting does not mutate transport events, the in-memory event
  journal, or the persisted result.
☐ Ensure a formatter failure becomes an explicit DwarfSpec CLI error without
  bypassing active-run abort and cleanup recovery.
☐ Preserve result persistence and acknowledgement after a normally formatted
  failed run.
☐ Preserve established exit codes for passing, failing, host-error, timeout,
  interruption, persistence-error, and recovery outcomes.

Phase 4 exit gate:

☐ Runner unit tests cover each selected format on streamed problem events.
☐ Runner unit tests prove regular progress remains present and ordered around
  the diagnostic line.
☐ Runner unit tests prove one event is emitted once across cursor polling.
☐ Runner unit tests prove formatted output does not alter the persisted
  structured event.
☐ CLI unit tests prove project configuration reaches the runner and invalid
  configuration fails before connecting to DFHack.
☐ Lua syntax checks and the full unit suite pass.

## Phase 5: Documentation and editor examples

Goal: document how projects select a format and pair it with an editor matcher.

☐ Add `settings.error_format` and its default to
  `docs/configuration.md`.
☐ Document the three configuration values and their exact output shapes.
☐ Document that `gcc` is also the Clang-compatible selection.
☐ Document the required problem matcher for each format.
☐ Document that `$gcc` is supplied by the Microsoft C/C++ extension rather
  than core VS Code.
☐ Document the absolute-path behavior for MSBuild and GCC/Clang and the
  project-relative behavior for ESLint Compact.
☐ Document the location-less human fallback and the fact that full traces
  remain in results and run logs.
☐ Update the repository's `tests/dwarfspec/config.lua` and Automation Test task
  so their selected format and problem matcher agree.
☐ Configure the repository task for one-shot execution only; do not add
  background matcher state.
☐ State that the task run clears previous Problems and that DwarfSpec does not
  manage Problems-panel retention or collapsing.
☐ Link to the official VS Code task and problem-matcher documentation.

Phase 5 exit gate:

☐ Documentation examples use valid DwarfSpec configuration syntax.
☐ The repository example pairs `msbuild` with `$mscompile`.
☐ Manual inspection confirms the task and configuration example agree.
☐ No automated test asserts VS Code task configuration or Problems-panel
  behavior.

## Phase 6: Completion verification

Goal: verify the complete DwarfSpec behavior and record reproducible evidence.

☐ Run the focused configuration, event, output-handler, report-formatter,
  runner, and CLI unit tests.
☐ Run `tools/Check-Lua.ps1`.
☐ Run `tools/Run-UnitTests.ps1`.
☐ Run `git diff --check`.
☐ Review the diff for accidental scheduler, transport, persistence, cleanup,
  exit-code, or in-game UI changes.
☐ Confirm all new or changed Lua functions and class-like modules have LuaDoc
  comments.
☐ Record exact commands, results, and any environmental limitations in the
  evidence ledger.

Phase 6 exit gate:

☐ All focused DwarfSpec unit tests pass.
☐ The full DwarfSpec unit suite passes.
☐ Lua syntax checks pass.
☐ `git diff --check` passes.
☐ Every earlier phase and exit gate is complete.
☐ No live DFHack run or VS Code integration test is required to complete this
  deterministic formatting work.

